  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1226 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nBroadly positive PV will prevail across much of the contiguous US \nduring the Thanksgiving week. This will favor the persistence of \ncool fall weather for our area, with temperatures forecast to hold \nabout 5 to 10 degrees cooler than average throughout much of the \nweek ahead.\n\nIn the wake of Sunday's cold front, with north winds still in \nprogress, a second reinforcing cold front is expected to arrive \nTuesday, knocking down temperatures further. A strong trough will be \npassing over the area atop the front around the same time. Rain \nchances of 20 to 30 percent are favored north of I-10 during the day \nTuesday. However, the cooler airmass and lack of substantial \nmoisture will limit keep rains light and stratiform in nature behind \nthe front. Temperatures aloft will be below freezing with a fairly \nsaturated mid-level layer, so given the dry layer underneath a few \nmodels are hinting at the possibility of a few ice pellets in this \nactivity possibly reaching the Hill Country. However, we do not \nexpect any impacts from this activity, and if any occurs it will \nlikely be more of a novelty and a hint that we aren't far away from \nwinter.\n\nTuesday's front will pave the path for a chilly leadup to \nThanksgiving. By Wednesday morning, most of the statistical guidance \nand deterministic guidance bring lows into the 30s across much of \nSouth-Central Texas, and a few spots in the Hill Country (including \nthe Llano, Burnet, and Fredericksburg areas) could experience their \nfirst light freeze of the season in the pre-dawn hours Wednesday. \nDespite mostly sunny conditions Wednesday and Thanksgiving Day, the \ncold air and northerly winds is expected to keep daytime \ntemperatures below 60F for most locations. Thanksgiving morning \nbrings another opportunity for light freezes in the Hill Country. \nThe latest national model blend shows about 20 to 40 percent of \nmodels projecting of freezing temperatures in the Hill Country \nWednesday and/or Thursday morning. Given the synoptic setup, I would \nnot be surprised if those odds increased. As a reminder, NWS \nAustin/San Antonio issues Freeze Warnings for counties forecast to \nexperience their first freeze of the season. Thus, if confidence \nincreases in that first freeze arriving, Freeze Warnings could be \nissued in a few days. \n\nLongwave troughing continues to be favored over much of the US after \nThanksgiving, keeping conditions cool.",
